          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Is it possible to draw uncountably many disjoint ‘figure eights’ in the plane?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p39-q20-definition",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two figure eights are said to be disjoint if the curves do not intersect, however, one curve may be contained within a lobe of the other.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p39-q20-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "No. Any collection of disjoint figure eights is countable.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p39-q20-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Choose one rational point inside each lobe. These pairs distinguish the figure eights and belong to the countable set ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\mathbb{Q}^{2}\\times\\mathbb{Q}^{2}",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ".",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $\\mathcal{F}$ be a collection of disjoint figure eights. Each lobe has a nonempty open interior, so it contains a point with rational coordinates. For each figure eight choose rational points $p$ and $q$, one in each lobe, and assign the pair $(p,q)\\in\\mathbb{Q}^{2}\\times\\mathbb{Q}^{2}$.\n\nTwo disjoint figure eights cannot have the same two points in separate lobes. If one figure eight lies inside a lobe of the other, both of its lobes lie inside that single lobe. Otherwise, their lobe interiors cannot overlap in a way that places the same two points in separate lobes of both figures without the curves intersecting. Thus the assignment is injective.\n\nThe rational numbers are countable, and a finite Cartesian product of countable sets is countable. Hence $\\mathbb{Q}^{2}\\times\\mathbb{Q}^{2}$ is countable. Since $\\mathcal{F}$ injects into this set, $\\mathcal{F}$ is countable. Therefore, uncountably many disjoint figure eights cannot be drawn.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
